### Why this works:
* **`canvas.width`**: This defines how many actual pixels are in the bitmap. By multiplying by `dpr`, you are creating a high-resolution image.
* **`canvas.style.width`**: This tells the browser how much space to occupy on the screen. Because the style size is smaller than the internal width, the browser performs a 1:1 pixel mapping instead of an upscaling stretch.
* **`ctx.scale(dpr, dpr)`**: Without this, if you told the canvas to draw a rectangle at `x: 10`, it would draw it at the 10th physical pixel (which is tiny). Scaling the context ensures that `x: 10` in your code maps to `x: 20` in the high-res buffer.

